### What is the success path for a payment?

The success path confirms a valid payment debits the payer, credits the merchant, records a transaction with a unique reference, and returns a confirmed status. This is the baseline against which all failure cases are compared.

```java
// PaymentSuccessTest.java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class PaymentSuccessTest {

    private final PaymentService paymentService = new PaymentService();

    @Test
    void successfulPaymentDebitsAndCreditsCorrectly() {
        PaymentRequest request = PaymentRequest.builder()
            .idempotencyKey("PAY-2024-001")
            .payerAccount("ACC-PRIYA-001")
            .merchantAccount("MERCHANT-NBI-001")
            .amount(new BigDecimal("50000.00"))
            .currency("INR")
            .build();

        PaymentResult result = paymentService.processPayment(request);

        assertEquals("CAPTURED", result.getStatus());
        assertNotNull(result.getTransactionId());
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("50000.00"), result.getAmount());
        // Verify balances
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("450000.00"),
            accountService.getBalance("ACC-PRIYA-001"));
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("50000.00"),
            accountService.getBalance("MERCHANT-NBI-001"));
    }
}
```

### What decline cases should you test?

Decline cases each produce a specific, safe outcome. The invariant is that a declined payment leaves balances completely untouched.

```java
// PaymentDeclineTest.java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class PaymentDeclineTest {

    @Test
    void insufficientBalanceRejectsWithoutPartialDebit() {
        BigDecimal originalBalance = accountService.getBalance("ACC-PRIYA-001");
        PaymentRequest request = PaymentRequest.builder()
            .amount(new BigDecimal("9999999.00"))
            .payerAccount("ACC-PRIYA-001")
            .build();

        PaymentResult result = paymentService.processPayment(request);

        assertEquals("DECLINED", result.getStatus());
        assertEquals("INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS", result.getErrorCode());
        // Balance must be completely untouched
        assertEquals(originalBalance,
            accountService.getBalance("ACC-PRIYA-001"));
    }

    @ParameterizedTest
    @CsvSource({
        "4111111111111111, 12/20, EXPIRED_CARD",
        "0000000000000000, 12/30, INVALID_CARD",
    })
    void invalidCardIsRejected(String cardNumber, String expiry,
                                String expectedError) {
        PaymentResult result = paymentService.processPayment(
            PaymentRequest.builder()
                .cardNumber(cardNumber)
                .expiry(expiry)
                .amount(new BigDecimal("1000.00"))
                .build()
        );
        assertEquals("DECLINED", result.getStatus());
        assertEquals(expectedError, result.getErrorCode());
    }
}
```

### What happens when a payment times out?

A payment timeout means the gateway did not respond in time, so the merchant does not know the outcome. The correct behavior is to treat the result as unknown and reconcile, never assuming failure, because the charge may have gone through.

```java
// PaymentTimeoutTest.java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class PaymentTimeoutTest {

    @Test
    void timeoutCreatesUnknownStatusForReconciliation() {
        // Simulate gateway timeout
        gatewayMock.setResponseDelay(30_000); // 30 seconds

        PaymentRequest request = PaymentRequest.builder()
            .idempotencyKey("PAY-TIMEOUT-001")
            .amount(new java.math.BigDecimal("5000.00"))
            .build();

        PaymentResult result = paymentService.processPayment(request);

        // Must NOT assume failure
        assertEquals("UNKNOWN", result.getStatus());
        // Must be queued for reconciliation
        assertTrue(reconciliationQueue.contains("PAY-TIMEOUT-001"));
    }
}
```

### Why does idempotency matter more than UI validation?

Idempotency and duplicate-transaction handling matter more than UI validation because of risk asymmetry. A UI validation bug produces an annoying but recoverable user error. A duplicate-charge bug moves real money wrongly, triggers chargebacks, regulatory exposure, and loss of trust.

```java
// IdempotencyTest.java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class IdempotencyTest {

    @Test
    void sameIdempotencyKeyProducesSingleCharge() {
        String key = "PAY-IDEMPOTENT-001";
        PaymentRequest request = PaymentRequest.builder()
            .idempotencyKey(key)
            .amount(new BigDecimal("10000.00"))
            .payerAccount("ACC-PRIYA-001")
            .build();

        PaymentResult first = paymentService.processPayment(request);
        PaymentResult second = paymentService.processPayment(request);
        PaymentResult third = paymentService.processPayment(request);

        // All three return the same transaction ID
        assertEquals(first.getTransactionId(), second.getTransactionId());
        assertEquals(first.getTransactionId(), third.getTransactionId());
        // Balance debited only once
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("490000.00"),
            accountService.getBalance("ACC-PRIYA-001"));
    }
}
```

### What is the payment-succeeds-but-confirmation-fails case?

This is the single most critical case. The gateway charged the customer, but the success response or webhook never reached the merchant. If the merchant naively retries, the customer is billed twice. If it marks the order failed, the customer paid for nothing.

```java
// ConfirmationFailureTest.java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class ConfirmationFailureTest {

    @Test
    void lostConfirmationDoesNotDoubleCharge() {
        String key = "PAY-LOST-CONFIRM-001";

        // First call succeeds at gateway but confirmation is lost
        gatewayMock.setConfirmationLost(true);
        PaymentResult first = paymentService.processPayment(
            PaymentRequest.builder()
                .idempotencyKey(key)
                .amount(new BigDecimal("25000.00"))
                .build()
        );
        assertEquals("UNKNOWN", first.getStatus());

        // Retry with same idempotency key
        gatewayMock.setConfirmationLost(false);
        PaymentResult retry = paymentService.processPayment(
            PaymentRequest.builder()
                .idempotencyKey(key)
                .amount(new BigDecimal("25000.00"))
                .build()
        );

        // Must return the original capture, not a new charge
        assertEquals("CAPTURED", retry.getStatus());
        // Balance debited only once
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("475000.00"),
            accountService.getBalance("ACC-PRIYA-001"));
    }
}
```

### What refund cases should you test?

Refund tests verify full refunds, partial refunds, and the invariant that total refunded never exceeds the captured amount. Refunds must also be idempotent.

```java
// RefundTest.java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class RefundTest {

    @Test
    void partialRefundReversesCorrectAmount() {
        String txnId = createSuccessfulPayment(new BigDecimal("50000.00"));

        RefundResult refund = paymentService.refund(txnId,
            new BigDecimal("20000.00"));

        assertEquals("REFUNDED", refund.getStatus());
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("20000.00"), refund.getRefundedAmount());
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("30000.00"), refund.getRemainingAmount());
    }

    @Test
    void refundExceedingCapturedAmountIsRejected() {
        String txnId = createSuccessfulPayment(new BigDecimal("10000.00"));

        assertThrows(RefundExceedsCaptureException.class, () ->
            paymentService.refund(txnId, new BigDecimal("15000.00"))
        );
    }

    @Test
    void duplicateRefundWithSameKeyIsIdempotent() {
        String txnId = createSuccessfulPayment(new BigDecimal("10000.00"));
        String refundKey = "REFUND-001";

        RefundResult first = paymentService.refund(txnId,
            new BigDecimal("5000.00"), refundKey);
        RefundResult second = paymentService.refund(txnId,
            new BigDecimal("5000.00"), refundKey);

        assertEquals(first.getRefundId(), second.getRefundId());
    }
}
```

### What happens when the user closes the browser mid-payment?

The user closing the browser mid-payment must not leave money in limbo. The server-side transaction state is the source of truth, not the browser.

```java
// BrowserCloseTest.java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class BrowserCloseTest {

    @Test
    void serverStateIsAuthoritativeWhenBrowserCloses() {
        String key = "PAY-BROWSER-CLOSE-001";

        // Payment initiated, browser closes before redirect
        PaymentRequest request = PaymentRequest.builder()
            .idempotencyKey(key)
            .amount(new java.math.BigDecimal("15000.00"))
            .build();

        paymentService.initiatePayment(request);
        // Simulate: browser closes, no callback received

        // Status check must reflect true gateway state
        PaymentStatus status = paymentService.checkStatus(key);
        assertNotNull(status);
        // If gateway captured, the order should be fulfilled
        // If gateway did not capture, the order is cancelled
        assertTrue(
            status.getStatus().equals("CAPTURED")
            || status.getStatus().equals("CANCELLED")
        );
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why not just validate the payment form UI?" Because a correct form can still double-charge, timeout, or lose a confirmation. The money-safety risks are at the API and integration level.
- "What is the difference between idempotency and deduplication?" Idempotency is enforced by the gateway using the key the client provides. Deduplication may also happen on the merchant side by checking transaction references. Both are needed.
- "How do you test currency conversion?" Assert that the rate is applied correctly, rounding uses the correct scale with no floating-point error, and both the charged and settled currencies are recorded.
